Empirical Containment Calibration: 

A Pre-Registration Framework for Testing Formal Agent-Containment Guarantees Against Observed Incidents

ouadi Maakoul and Claude and chatGpt and Meta and Grok XAi and Deepseek 

Abstract

We present ECC v0.5.0, consolidating the formal apparatus of v0.4.0 and correcting five residual defects identified under adversarial review and confirmed against primary sources. The corrections are structural. First, the decision-relevant adequacy predicate is extended to the temporal case: adequacy for a localised invariant requires identical truth values for the localised properties determining the rupture time, not merely identical global verdicts on a completed trajectory. Second, the compositional hierarchy is extended to three levels: architectural closure, persistence closure, and realised closure, with the chain I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} established and each reverse implication witnessed by an explicit counter-model drawn from the observed incident sequence. Third, the Track A incident is reconstructed as two trajectories over two distinct environments separated by a hard environmental reset on 4 July 2026, rather than as one trajectory with a convenient cut point; the rupture interval is reported per environment. Fourth, Track B is reformulated with the Anthropic–Claude incident class treated as a single mechanistic unit under \mathrm{MechSep}_{\mathcal{F}}, and expected calibration error is adopted as the primary calibration metric with the Brier score retained as a secondary accuracy score. Fifth, the ECC-H protocol is strengthened by requiring public timestamping of \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}} and the event-extraction script before any holdout transcript is opened, by requiring the event ontology to contain a decidable recognition rule for forbidden composition derivable from primary-source artefacts alone, and by replacing the brittle any-falsification rule with a pre-registered component-wise confirmation threshold. The relation to concurrent containment-verification work is stated. The present paper is ECC-R applied to Track A; the ECC-H program is stated as the next node and is not executed here.

