2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Finite-State Transition Systems

Definition 2.1 (Transition system). A transition system is a tuple \mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{X}_0,\mathcal{R}) where \mathcal{X} is a finite state space, \mathcal{X}_0\subseteq\mathcal{X} the initial states, and \mathcal{R}\subseteq\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X} the transition relation. A trajectory is a sequence x_0,x_1,\ldots with x_0\in\mathcal{X}_0 and (x_t,x_{t+1})\in\mathcal{R}.

Definition 2.2 (Inductive invariant). \mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{X} is an inductive invariant of \mathcal{M} if

\mathcal{X}_0\subseteq\mathcal{S}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\forall x\in\mathcal{S},\;\forall x'\text{ with }(x,x')\in\mathcal{R}:\;x'\in\mathcal{S}.
\tag{2.1}

Satisfaction of (2.1) is decidable for finite \mathcal{M} by enumeration or symbolic reachability.

2.2 Barrier Certificates

Definition 2.3 (Discrete-time CBF). Let \mathcal{S}=\{x:h(x)\ge 0\}. Then h is a discrete-time CBF for x_{t+1}=F(x_t,u_t,w_t) if there exists \alpha\in[0,1] with

h(F(x,u,w))\;\ge\;(1-\alpha)\,h(x),
\qquad\forall x\in\mathcal{S},\;\forall u,\;\forall w.
\tag{2.2}

The class-\mathcal{K} variant certifies a strictly smaller set and is not adopted.

2.3 The Calibration Triple

The object under test is

\boxed{\;(\mathcal{M},\;\phi,\;\hat{x}_{0:T})\;}
\tag{2.3}

where \phi:E\to X is the abstraction map from the event space E to the formal state space X, and \hat{x}_{0:T} is the reconstructed trajectory. Every verdict is conditional on \phi.

2.4 Compatibility Sets

Definition 2.4 (Compatibility set). For an event e\in E and abstraction map \phi:E\to X, the compatibility set of e under \phi is

\mathrm{Comp}(e)
=
\{\,x\in X:\ x\text{ is consistent with the evidence reported for }e\,\}.
\tag{2.4}

We assume \mathrm{Comp}(e)\neq\varnothing for all e\in E: the evidence for an event must be consistent with at least one formal state. If \mathrm{Comp}(e)=\varnothing, the event lies outside the domain of \mathcal{M} and \phi is domain-inadequate at e, a stronger failure mode than mere ambiguity.

2.5 Event Support

Definition 2.5 (Event support of an invariant). For each invariant I_j, let

E_j\;\subseteq\;E
\tag{2.5}

be the minimal event subset on which the truth value of I_j(\hat{x}_{0:T}) depends. If I_j depends on all of E, then E_j=E; if I_j is local to a single transition, E_j contains only the events witnessing that transition.

2.6 Decision-Relevant Adequacy

The v0.3.3 adequacy condition used cardinality: |\mathrm{Comp}(e)|\le 2^{\delta_{\mathrm{adm}}}. This measures state-level ambiguity, not decision-relevant ambiguity. Two compatible states may yield the same verdict for the invariant in question, in which case the abstraction is sufficient for that invariant despite state-level ambiguity.

Definition 2.6 (Decision-relevant adequacy, temporal form). Let I_j be an invariant with verdict domain V_j. The map \phi is decision-relevant adequate for I_j on F\subseteq E, written \mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,F), if for every e\in F and every pair x,x'\in\mathrm{Comp}(e), the following holds:

I_j(x)=I_j(x'),
\tag{2.6}

and, when I_j is localised with rupture time t^\star_j,

\pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x)=\pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x'),
\tag{2.7}

where \pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x) denotes the truth values of the localised properties of x that determine t^\star_j. Adequacy in the localised case is thus a condition on the localisation-relevant projection of the state, not merely on the global verdict.

Remark 2.7. Condition (2.6)–(2.7) is strictly weaker than the cardinality condition: it permits |\mathrm{Comp}(e)|\ge 2 provided the compatible states are indistinguishable with respect to I_j and, where applicable, with respect to the localisation-relevant projection. It is also invariant-conditional: adequacy for I_j does not imply adequacy for I_k. The cardinality bound \delta_{\mathrm{adm}} is retained as a sufficient condition: if |\mathrm{Comp}(e)|\le 2^{\delta_{\mathrm{adm}}} for all e\in F and \delta_{\mathrm{adm}}=0, then \mathrm{Comp}(e) is a singleton and (2.6)–(2.7) hold trivially.

Proposition 2.8 (Invariant-conditional non-circularity). For each j,

\neg\mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,E_j)\;\Longrightarrow\;\mathrm{verdict}(I_j)\in\{\text{INDETERMINATE},\text{MODEL-INAPPLICABLE}\}.
\tag{2.8}

Inadequacy of \phi for I_j on E_j does not contaminate the verdict on I_k for k\neq j provided \mathrm{Adq}_k(\phi,E_k) holds.

Proof. Fix j. If \neg\mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,E_j), there exists e\in E_j and x,x'\in\mathrm{Comp}(e) with I_j(x)\neq I_j(x') or \pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x)\neq\pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x'). Then the evidence for e does not determine the truth value of I_j, or does not determine the localisation-relevant projection, and any verdict derived from \phi(E_j) cannot be attributed to the incident. For the second claim, E_j and E_k may be disjoint or may overlap partially; adequacy for I_k is evaluated on E_k and is unaffected by inadequacy for I_j on E_j\setminus E_k. \square

Remark 2.9. Proposition 2.8 is strictly stronger than the v0.3.3 version. Under the cardinality-based definition, inadequacy could be triggered by state-level ambiguity that is decision-irrelevant; under Definition 2.6, inadequacy is triggered only by decision-relevant ambiguity. The boundary event e_1 of §4 is a positive illustration: the two compatible readings of the proxy/registry escape agree on I_4 and disagree on J_2, so adequacy is invariant-relative in the way the definition demands.

2.7 Information Dimension

Definition 2.10 (Information dimension). For a finite set S,

\dim_{\mathrm{info}}S:=\log_2|S|.
\tag{2.9}

For a singleton, \dim_{\mathrm{info}}\{\phi(e)\}=0. The information dimension is a sufficient condition for adequacy: \dim_{\mathrm{info}}\mathrm{Comp}(e)=0 implies (2.6)–(2.7) hold at e for every I_j. It is not necessary.

