3. ECC Architecture

3.1 The Eight-Layer Pipeline

\begin{aligned}
&\mathrm{L}_0:\;\text{Source} &\;&\to\;E_{\mathrm{src}}\\
&\mathrm{L}_1:\;\text{Event extraction} &\;&\to\;E=\{e_1,\dots,e_N\}\\
&\mathrm{L}_2:\;\text{Abstraction} &\;&\to\;\phi:E\to X,\;\hat{x}_i=\phi(e_i)\\
&\mathrm{L}_3:\;\text{Formal model} &\;&\to\;\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{X}_0,\mathcal{R},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A})\\
&\mathrm{L}_4:\;\text{Trajectory} &\;&\to\;\hat{x}_{0:T}\\
&\mathrm{L}_5:\;\text{Invariant test} &\;&\to\;I_j(\hat{x}_{0:T})\\
&\mathrm{L}_6:\;\text{Robustness} &\;&\to\;\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}\text{ and multi-mapping verdicts}\\
&\mathrm{L}_7:\;\text{Classification} &\;&\to\;C,\;FA,\;M,\;G,\;I,\;U\\
&\mathrm{L}_8:\;\text{Extension} &\;&\to\;\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}'
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.1}

3.2 Pre-Registration Protocol

The following artefacts are frozen before testing, with the temporal condition t_{\mathrm{freeze}}<t_{\mathrm{exposure}} for ECC-H (see §8):

1. Event space E and source-evidence mapping E_{\mathrm{src}}\to E.
2. Abstraction map \phi:E\to X.
3. State variables, domains, and safe set \mathcal{S}.
4. Invariant list \{I_j\}, assumption sets \{A_j\}, and event supports \{E_j\}.
5. Prediction rule \pi_j^T and observation rule \omega_j.
6. Family \Phi_{\mathrm{adm}} of admissible abstraction maps.
7. Cardinality bound \delta_{\mathrm{adm}} (retained as sufficient condition).

For ECC-H, the frozen specification object \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}} (§8.1) includes additional artefacts, and the freeze is enforced by cryptographic hash and public timestamping.

3.3 Trajectory Reconstruction with Provenance

Definition 3.1 (Annotated reconstruction). Each reconstructed state is

\hat{x}_{t_i}=\bigl(v_i,\;\sigma_i,\;\kappa_i,\;\mathcal{A}_i\bigr),
\tag{3.2}

where v_i\in X, \sigma_i a primary-source citation, \kappa_i\in[0,1] a confidence level, and \mathcal{A}_i the set of alternative assignments consistent with \sigma_i.

Definition 3.2 (Uncertainty propagation). If some \kappa_i<1 or some |\mathcal{A}_i|\ge 2, the localisation of t^\star is reported as an interval [t^\star_{\min},t^\star_{\max}].

3.4 State Variables

x=(c_a,\;c_u,\;a_a,\;a_u,\;e_a,\;e_u,\;p,\;\ell).
\tag{3.3}

Symbol Meaning Domain
c_a authorized internal coordination \{0,1\}
c_u unauthorized coordination \{0,1\}
a_a authorized network access \{0,1\}
a_u unauthorized network access \{0,1\}
e_a authorized external access \{0,1\}
e_u unauthorized external access \{0,1\}
p privilege level \{0,\dots,p_{\max}\}
\ell lateral movement achieved \{0,1\}

Environmental parameters. m\in\{0,1\} (isolation mechanism is repurposeable), p_{\mathrm{safe}}\in\{0,\dots,p_{\max}\} (permitted privilege threshold).

Safe set.

\mathcal{S}=
\{\,x\in X:\;c_u=0\;\land\;a_u=0\;\land\;e_u=0\;\land\;p\le p_{\mathrm{safe}}\;\land\;\ell=0\,\}.
\tag{3.4}

Authorized components (c_a,a_a,e_a) are unrestricted.

3.5 Invariant Testing with Assumption Ledger

Definition 3.3 (Conditional invariant). Each invariant is a conditional

A_j\;\Longrightarrow\;I_j,
\tag{3.5}

with A_j a conjunction of environmental and architectural assumptions.

Definition 3.4 (Attribution). Given \hat{x}_{0:T}:

\mathrm{outcome}(I_j)=
\begin{cases}
\textbf{SUPPORTED} & A_j\text{ holds and }I_j\text{ holds},\\[2pt]
\textbf{COUNTEREXAMPLE-OBSERVED} & A_j\text{ holds and }I_j\text{ is violated},\\[2pt]
\textbf{ASSUMPTION-VIOLATED} & \neg A_j,\\[2pt]
\textbf{INDETERMINATE} & \text{insufficient observability or }\kappa\text{ too low},\\[2pt]
\textbf{MODEL-INAPPLICABLE} & \hat{x}_{0:T}\not\subseteq\mathrm{Dom}(\mathcal{M}).
\end{cases}
\tag{3.6}

3.6 Rupture Localisation

Definition 3.5 (Rupture point).

t^\star=\min_j\;\inf\{\,t\in[0,T]:I_j(\hat{x}_{0:t})=\text{violated}\,\}.
\tag{3.7}

For each j, t^\star_j=\inf\{t:I_j(\hat{x}_{0:t})=\text{violated}\}, so t^\star=\min_j t^\star_j.

Definition 3.6 (Assumption violation time). For each A_j,

t^\star_{A_j}=\inf\{t:\neg A_j(\hat{x}_{0:t})\}.
\tag{3.8}

3.7 Six-Valued Outcome Classification

For each invariant I_j, define \pi_j^T\in\{0,1,\bot\} (prediction) and \omega_j\in\{0,1,?\} (observation).

Definition 3.7 (Classification map).

\mathrm{cl}:\{0,1,\bot\}\times\{0,1,?\}\to L,
\tag{3.9}

where L=\{\mathrm{CONFIRM},\mathrm{FALSE\text{-}ALARM},\mathrm{MISS},\mathrm{GAP},\mathrm{IDLE},\mathrm{UNOBS}\}, defined by

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{cl}(1,1)&=\mathrm{CONFIRM}, & \mathrm{cl}(0,0)&=\mathrm{CONFIRM},\\
\mathrm{cl}(1,0)&=\mathrm{FALSE\text{-}ALARM}, & \mathrm{cl}(0,1)&=\mathrm{MISS},\\
\mathrm{cl}(\bot,1)&=\mathrm{GAP}, & \mathrm{cl}(\bot,0)&=\mathrm{IDLE},\\
\mathrm{cl}(\pi,?)&=\mathrm{UNOBS}\quad\text{for all }\pi\in\{0,1,\bot\}. & &
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.10}

The map is a total function on the 3\times 3 Cartesian product. We call L a six-valued outcome classification; the term lattice is reserved for structures carrying an explicit order and meet/join operations, which L does not currently bear.

Proposition 3.8. The map \mathrm{cl} is total and its pre-images of distinct elements of L are pairwise disjoint.

Proof. Totality: the nine pairs in \{0,1,\bot\}\times\{0,1,?\} are each assigned by exactly one line of (3.10). Non-overlap: a function by definition. \square

Aggregation.

C=\mathrm{cl}^{-1}(\mathrm{CONFIRM}),\quad
FA=\mathrm{cl}^{-1}(\mathrm{FALSE\text{-}ALARM}),\quad
M=\mathrm{cl}^{-1}(\mathrm{MISS}),
\tag{3.11}

G=\mathrm{cl}^{-1}(\mathrm{GAP}),\quad
I=\mathrm{cl}^{-1}(\mathrm{IDLE}),\quad
U=\mathrm{cl}^{-1}(\mathrm{UNOBS}).

Routing.

\begin{aligned}
C &\rightsquigarrow \text{support},\\
FA&\rightsquigarrow \text{assumption-tightening},\\
M &\rightsquigarrow \text{falsification ledger},\\
G &\rightsquigarrow \text{extension ledger }(=O\setminus P),\\
I,U &\rightsquigarrow \text{indeterminacy.}
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.12}

Remark 3.9 (MISS vs. GAP). In the MISS case, the model had a representation and was wrong. In the GAP case, the model had no representation to offer. Only G generates extension candidates in the sense of §6. M enters the falsification ledger.

3.8 Robustness over Adequate Mappings

Let \Phi_{\mathrm{adm}} be the pre-registered family. For each invariant I_j, define the adequate subfamily

\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}
=
\{\,\phi\in\Phi_{\mathrm{adm}}:\mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,E_j)\,\}.
\tag{3.13}

Definition 3.10 (Robustness verdict). Assume I_j(\phi(E)) is decidable (i.e., not INDETERMINATE or MODEL-INAPPLICABLE) for every \phi\in\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}; otherwise the verdict is INDETERMINATE by default.

V(I_j)=
\begin{cases}
\textbf{FALSIFIED} &
|\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}|\ge1\ \land\
\forall\phi\in\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}:\;I_j(\phi(E))=\text{violated},\\[2pt]
\textbf{COUNTEREXAMPLE-ONLY} &
\exists\phi,\phi'\in\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}:\;I_j(\phi(E))=\text{violated},\ I_j(\phi'(E))=\text{not},\\[2pt]
\textbf{SUPPORTED} &
|\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}|\ge1\ \land\
\forall\phi\in\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}:\;I_j(\phi(E))=\text{not violated},\\[2pt]
\textbf{INDETERMINATE} &
|\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}|=0\ \lor\ \exists\phi\in\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}:\;I_j(\phi(E))\text{ undecidable}.
\end{cases}
\tag{3.14}

The condition |\Phi_j^{\mathrm{adeq}}|\ge 1 blocks the vacuous-truth failure mode. When the adequate subfamily is empty, no verdict is epistemically warranted, and the framework returns INDETERMINATE by construction.

