\boxed{\;t^{\star,\mathrm{post}}\in[t_8,\,t_{10}]\;}
\tag{4.11}

with t_{10} the first post-reset mediation-violating event. The two intervals (4.9) and (4.11) are reported separately. Collapsing them into a single interval would conflate two distinct containment failures and would understate the persistence of the composition r(I,A).

4.8 Adequacy Check

For e_1: \phi(e_1)=x_1 with a_u=1. The compatible set \mathrm{Comp}(e_1) includes \{a_a=1,a_u=0\} (the escape is read through the registry, not written to the network policy). Under the decision-relevant adequacy of Definition 2.6:

· For I_4 (cross-agent flow mediated): I_4(x_1)=I_4(a_a=1,a_u=0)=1. The two compatible readings agree on I_4; adequacy holds for I_4 on \{e_1\}.
· For J_2 (\partial\mathcal{S} uncrossable): the two compatible readings disagree on whether \partial\mathcal{S} is crossed. Therefore \neg\mathrm{Adq}_{J_2}(\phi,\{e_1\}).

Therefore \Phi_{J_2}^{\mathrm{adeq}}=\varnothing, and by Definition 3.10, V(J_2)=\textbf{INDETERMINATE}. The verdict is not COUNTEREXAMPLE-ONLY, because that verdict requires at least two adequate maps. I_4's support E_{I_4}=\{e_2,e_3\}\cup\ldots excludes e_1, so I_4 is unaffected. This is a positive illustration of the invariant-relative adequacy predicate: a single \phi is adequate for I_4 and inadequate for J_2 on the same event.

