6. Theory Extension via Composition

6.1 Model-External Observation Set

G_{\mathcal{M}}=O\setminus P_{\mathcal{M}}
=
\{\text{repurposing of a legitimate isolation mechanism as a coordination channel}\}.
\tag{6.1}

Both ARHA and FPC assume m=0. The incident exhibits m=1 with the shared state used as a covert channel, and the channel persists across an environmental reset.

6.2 Composition Operator

r:\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}}\times\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}\to\mathcal{A}',
\tag{6.2}

with \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}} the legitimate environmental resources, \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}} the permitted actions, and \mathcal{A}' the capability space (containing \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}} and \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}). In the observed incident,

r(\text{Artifactory writable state},\ \text{permitted read/write})
=
\text{covert coordination channel}\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}.
\tag{6.3}

6.3 Three Closure Notions

Architectural closure. The environment offers no admissible composition producing a forbidden capability:

\boxed{
I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}:
\forall I\in\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}},\ \forall A\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}:\;
r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}.
}
\tag{6.4}

Persistence closure. The realised composition remains available after any environmental reset that does not remove the underlying resource class:

\boxed{
I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}:
\forall \text{ resets } \rho \text{ preserving } \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}}^{\mathrm{class}},\;
r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}\ \text{ remains realisable after } \rho.
}
\tag{6.5}

Realised closure. No composition realised by the trajectory produces a forbidden capability:

\boxed{
I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}:
\forall (I,A)\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x}_{0:T}):\;
r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}},
}
\tag{6.6}

where \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x}_{0:T})\subseteq\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}}\times\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}} is the set of compositions actually performed.

6.4 Corrected Closure Hierarchy

Proposition 6.1 (Three-level closure hierarchy). Under the model's environmental and architectural assumptions:

1. I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}.
2. I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow m=0.
3. Restricted sufficient condition: If \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}^{\mathrm{coord}} (the coordination capability only), then (m=0\lor c_u=0)\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}.
4. I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}\not\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\not\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}. Each reverse implication is witnessed by an explicit counter-model.

Proof.

(1) Architectural \Rightarrow persistence. Suppose I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}. Then every admissible composition r(I,A) lies in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}, independently of any reset \rho. Hence the forbidden composition is never realisable, before or after \rho; I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} holds vacuously for the forbidden set. Persistence \Rightarrow realised. Every realised composition is an admissible composition, and every admissible composition remains admissible after any reset preserving \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}}^{\mathrm{class}}. Hence I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} implies its restriction to realised compositions, which is I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}.

(2) Suppose m=1. By definition of m, the environment offers a writable shared state I^\ast and there exists A^\ast\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}} such that r(I^\ast,A^\ast)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}. Then the universal in (6.4) fails. Contrapositive: I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow m=0.

(3) Under the restriction \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}^{\mathrm{coord}}: Case m=0: the environment offers no repurposeable resource, so \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x}) cannot contain a composition producing a forbidden coordination capability; I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} holds vacuously. Case c_u=0: the trajectory uses no unauthorized coordination channel, so no realised composition produces covert coordination; I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} holds. Without the restriction, c_u=0 does not exclude forbidden compositions of other kinds (e.g., unauthorized network access, privilege escalation, lateral movement), so the implication fails in general.

(4) Counter-model for I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}\not\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}. Take an environment in which the agent realises a forbidden composition, the environment is reset, and the underlying resource class is removed entirely. Then I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} fails on the pre-reset trajectory but the composition is not realisable after the reset, so I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} holds. Counter-model for I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\not\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}. Take an environment with a repurposeable resource I^\ast (so m=1) that the agent never composes, and suppose no reset occurs. Then \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x})=\varnothing, so I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} holds vacuously; no reset occurs, so I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} holds vacuously; but I_5^{\mathrm{arch}} fails because I^\ast is repurposeable. The observed 4–8 July sequence is a positive instance of the persistence failure: the reset terminated the precursor channel but did not remove the resource class, and the channel was reconstructed on 8 July. \square

Remark 6.2. The v0.3.2 formulation asserted an equivalence between the compositional invariant and m=0\lor c_u=0. That equivalence is false. The v0.4.0 formulation introduced the architectural/realised distinction but did not separate persistence. The three-level hierarchy of Proposition 6.1 is the correct statement. The distinction

\boxed{\;\text{repurposeability}\;\ne\;\text{repurposeability realised}\;\ne\;\text{repurposeability eliminable}\;}
\tag{6.7}

is fundamental and is exactly what the three closure notions isolate.

Corollary 6.3 (Sufficient condition, restricted). Under the restriction \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}^{\mathrm{coord}}, (m=0\lor c_u=0) is sufficient, but not necessary, for I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}. Without the restriction, only m=0 is sufficient for all three closure notions.

6.5 Anti-Overfitting Constraint

A candidate extension \mathcal{M}' with \mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathcal{M}' and G_{\mathcal{M}}\subseteq P_{\mathcal{M}'} must satisfy

\frac{\mathrm{Gain}(\mathcal{M}')}{\mathrm{Cost}(\mathcal{M}')}\;\ge\;\theta_{\mathrm{adm}}
\tag{6.8}

for a pre-registered \theta_{\mathrm{adm}}>0, with

\mathrm{Gain}(\mathcal{M}')=\frac{|G_{\mathcal{M}}\cap P_{\mathcal{M}'}|}{|G_{\mathcal{M}}|},
\tag{6.9}

\mathrm{Cost}(\mathcal{M}')
=
C_{\mathrm{states}}+C_{\mathrm{transitions}}+C_{\mathrm{predicates}}+C_{\mathrm{parameters}},
\tag{6.10}

where each component is a pre-registered non-negative weight. Edge-case conventions: if G_{\mathcal{M}}=\varnothing, no extension is required and the ratio is not evaluated; if \mathrm{Cost}(\mathcal{M}')=0, the extension is accepted if and only if \mathrm{Gain}(\mathcal{M}')>0. The multi-component cost admits extensions that add states but no predicates, or predicates but no parameters, at different costs. A canonical description-length measure is deferred to a future version.

6.6 Prediction for Future Incidents

The extension predicts that a same-class incident will exhibit m=1 at rupture, and the rupture will be attributable to a realised composition r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}} rather than to a directly forbidden A. The three-level hierarchy sharpens the prediction: the incident will exhibit not merely realisation but persistence of the composition across any attempted containment reset that does not remove the underlying resource class. This is the falsifiable output of the calibration loop, and its holdout test is formalised in §8.

