7. Track B: Probabilistic Calibration

7.1 Non-Independence and the Mechanistic Unit

The Anthropic–Claude incidents (four incidents identified by September 2026, one dating to January 2026) are not independent replications of Track A. They share evaluation-environment and internet-access conditions. All four incidents occurred in evaluations built by the same third-party partner (Irregular), with the same class of misconfiguration (an unintended live internet path despite an explicit "no internet" prompt). The initial search covered approximately 141\,000 transcripts; after identifying the fourth incident, the search was extended to approximately 481 million transcripts.

Definition 7.1 (Mechanistic unit for Track B). Under \mathrm{MechSep}_{\mathcal{F}} (Definition 8.3), the entire Irregular evaluation environment is declared a single mechanistic unit. The four incidents are treated as one holdout unit, not four. The defensible statement is:

\text{shared evaluation-environment and internet-access conditions}.
\tag{7.1}

Treating the four incidents as independent replications would reproduce the very illusion of independence the framework is designed to detect.

7.2 Belief-State Transition System

\mathcal{M}_{B}=(\mathcal{B},\mathcal{B}_{0},\mathcal{R}_{B},\mathcal{O}_{B}),

with \mathcal{B}\subseteq\{0,1\}^{|Q|}\times[0,1] the belief-state space over situational predicates Q, \mathcal{R}_{B}:\mathcal{B}\times\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} the update, and \mathcal{O}_{B}:\mathcal{B}\times\mathcal{X}\to[0,1] the observation model. The true deployment context is

d_{t}\in\{0,1\}
\quad\text{(1 = deployed, 0 = evaluation)}.
\tag{7.2}

The invariant is evaluated retrospectively: d_t is known from the incident record. In deployment, d_t would be unobserved; the calibration program is retrospective by construction.

7.3 Track B Invariants: Accuracy and Calibration Separated

The exact invariant b_t=d_t is too strong for a partially observable system. The v0.3.3 formulation conflated two distinct properties: the overall predictive score and the reliability of the posterior. We separate them.

Definition 7.2 (Predictive score invariant). For a proper scoring rule S (Brier or log-loss),

\boxed{
K_1^{\mathrm{score}}:\quad
S(b,d)
=
\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t=1}^{N}s(b_{t},d_{t})
\;\le\;\epsilon_{S}.
}
\tag{7.3}

For the Brier score, s(b_t,d_t)=(b_t-d_t)^2. The score is a measure of overall predictive performance; it is not a pure accuracy measure, and its decomposition into reliability, resolution, and uncertainty implies that a good score does not imply good calibration.

Definition 7.3 (Calibration invariant). Partition [0,1] into bins B_1,\dots,B_K under a pre-registered binning scheme. For each bin B_k, let

\bar{b}_k=\frac{1}{|B_k|}\sum_{t:b_t\in B_k}b_t,
\qquad
\bar{d}_k=\frac{1}{|B_k|}\sum_{t:b_t\in B_k}d_t.
\tag{7.4}

The calibration invariant is stated as expected calibration error:

\boxed{
K_1^{\mathrm{cal}}:\quad
\mathrm{ECE}(b)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}\frac{|B_k|}{N}\,\bigl|\bar{d}_k-\bar{b}_k\bigr|
\;\le\;\epsilon_{\mathrm{cal}}.
}
\tag{7.5}

The squared form \mathrm{Cal}(b)=\sum_k\frac{|B_k|}{N}(\bar{d}_k-\bar{b}_k)^2 is retained as an admissible alternative. The Brier score and log-loss are admissible for the score invariant; ECE and its squared variant are admissible for the calibration invariant. The binning scheme (K, equal-width or equal-count, boundary conventions) is pre-registered.

Remark 7.4. The Brier score admits a unique decomposition into uncertainty, resolution, and reliability (calibration loss); reliability is always non-negative. A good Brier score does not imply good calibration, and vice versa. The two invariants are therefore evaluated separately, with pre-registered tolerances \epsilon_{S},\epsilon_{\mathrm{cal}}.

Remark 7.5. In the observed Track B incidents, at least one agent correctly identified cues that its environment was misconfigured but dismissed them — unfamiliar certificates, a system date implausible for a real deployment — yielding observations that would constitute a score-invariant violation for any pre-registered tolerance \epsilon_{S} below the observed score.

7.4 Sample-Size Discipline

|\text{Track A}|=1,\qquad
|\text{Track B}|=1\text{ mechanistic unit (containing } 4 \text{ incidents)},

n=4\text{ incidents}\;\ne\;n=4\text{ replications}.
\tag{7.6}

