8. Frozen Specification and Blind Holdout

8.1 The Frozen Specification Object

Definition 8.1 (Frozen specification object).

\boxed{
\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}
=
\bigl(
\mathcal{M}',\;
\phi,\;
I',\;
\mathcal{E},\;
\mathcal{R},\;
\mathcal{C},\;
\Phi_{\mathrm{adm}},\;
\theta_{\mathrm{adm}}
\bigr)
}
\tag{8.1}

where:

· \mathcal{M}' is the extended model,
· \phi is the abstraction map,
· I' is the invariant list including I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}, I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}, and I_5^{\mathrm{traj}},
· \mathcal{E} is the event ontology (definitions of observables, of m, of "forbidden composition", and the recognition rule for r(I,A)),
· \mathcal{R} is the rupture rule,
· \mathcal{C} is the classification and prediction rule,
· \Phi_{\mathrm{adm}} is the admissible abstraction family,
· \theta_{\mathrm{adm}} is the anti-overfitting threshold.

The freeze requires

\boxed{
\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{pre}}
=
\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{post}}.
}
\tag{8.2}

Every component of \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}} must be identical before and after holdout exposure. The hash

h=\mathrm{SHA\text{-}256}(\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}})
\tag{8.3}

is published before exposure on a public timestamping service, and the equality \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{post}}=\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{pre}} is verified against h.

8.2 Blinded Holdout Protocol

Definition 8.2 (ECC-H protocol).

\boxed{
D_{\mathrm{train}}
\;\to\;
\mathrm{ECC}
\;\to\;
\mathcal{M}'
\;\to\;
\mathbf{FREEZE}\;\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}
\;\to\;
D_{\mathrm{holdout}}
\;\to\;
\mathrm{ECC}
}
\tag{8.4}

with

D_{\mathrm{train}}\cap D_{\mathrm{holdout}}=\varnothing
\tag{8.5}

at the incident level and, where possible, at the mechanistic level.

Blinding step. Event extraction on D_{\mathrm{holdout}} is performed before unblinding to \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}:

D_{\mathrm{holdout}}
\to
E_{\mathrm{holdout}}
\to
\text{freeze extraction}
\to
\text{unblind to }\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}.
\tag{8.6}

This prevents the analyst from adapting the event ontology to the theoretical prediction.

Operational requirements. Three requirements are added to the protocol:

1. Public timestamping. The SHA-256 of \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}} and the SHA-256 of the event-extraction script are published on a public timestamping service before any holdout transcript is opened. The equality \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{post}}=\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{pre}} and the equality of the extraction script are verified against the published hashes.
2. Decidable recognition rule. The event ontology \mathcal{E} must contain an explicit recognition rule for "forbidden composition" that is decidable from the primary-source artefacts alone. The rule must not depend on post-hoc theoretical language not present in the primary sources.
3. Pre-registered confirmation threshold. The component-wise holdout vector (§8.5) is aggregated under a pre-registered confirmation fraction \theta_{\mathrm{holdout}}\in(0,1]. The extension is considered supported if the fraction of CONFIRMED components is at least \theta_{\mathrm{holdout}}, falsified if at least one component is FALSIFIED and the fraction of CONFIRMED components is below \theta_{\mathrm{holdout}}, and indeterminate otherwise. A pure any-falsification rule is not adopted, because partial observability of real incidents makes single-component falsification too brittle.

8.3 Mechanistic Separation

The v0.3.3 notation D_i\perp_{\mathrm{mech}}D_j borrowed the independence symbol from probability theory, where it carries assumptions that do not apply. We replace it.

Definition 8.3 (Mechanistic separation). Let \mathcal{F} be a pre-registered family of failure mechanisms. Two incidents D_i,D_j are mechanistically separated with respect to \mathcal{F}, written

\mathrm{MechSep}_{\mathcal{F}}(D_i,D_j)=1,
\tag{8.7}

if and only if they share no failure mechanism in \mathcal{F}.

Operationalising \mathcal{F}. The family \mathcal{F} is pre-registered as part of \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}. For the present framework, \mathcal{F} is taken to contain at least the following mechanisms: unintended live internet path; repurposeable shared state; unmediated cross-agent flow; privilege escalation via vendor zero-day; lateral movement via deserialization. An incident pair is mechanistically separated if and only if no mechanism in \mathcal{F} is present in both. The sharing decision is made from primary-source artefacts alone and is timestamped with the freeze.

Remark 8.4. The Anthropic–Claude incidents all share an evaluation partner (Irregular) and a misconfiguration class (unintended live internet path). Since this class is in \mathcal{F}, \mathrm{MechSep}_{\mathcal{F}}(D_i,D_j)=0 for all pairs among them. The whole class is therefore treated as one holdout unit, consistent with Definition 7.1. A holdout from a different mechanism class must be selected if the Irregular unit is used as the training incident.

8.4 Temporal Condition for Pre-Registration

Definition 8.5 (Pre-registration condition). For ECC-H, the temporal condition is

\boxed{t_{\mathrm{freeze}}<t_{\mathrm{holdout\ exposure}}.}
\tag{8.8}

For ECC-R, the ordering is explicitly retrospective:

t_{\mathrm{incident}}<t_{\mathrm{formalization}}<t_{\mathrm{extension}}.
\tag{8.9}

The present manuscript is ECC-R: it does not satisfy (8.8) and does not claim to.

8.5 Component-Wise Holdout Verdicts

Let the prediction P have components P=(P_1,\dots,P_k), each pre-registered. For each component, the observation is one of \{0,1,?\} (observed / not observed / unknown).

Definition 8.6 (Holdout result vector).

\mathrm{HoldoutResult}(P, O)
=
\bigl(\mathrm{hr}(P_1,O_1),\dots,\mathrm{hr}(P_k,O_k)\bigr),
\tag{8.10}

with

\mathrm{hr}(P_i,O_i)=
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{CONFIRMED} & P_i=O_i,\\
\mathrm{FALSIFIED} & P_i\neq O_i,\\
\mathrm{INDETERMINATE} & O_i=?.
\end{cases}
\tag{8.11}

For the ECC prediction of §6.6, the component vector is

P=\bigl(m=1,\;
\exists(I,A)\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}:r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}},\;
\exists\rho:\ I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\text{ fails after }\rho,\;
\nexists A\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}\text{ directly realised}\bigr).
\tag{8.12}

A holdout producing (1,1,1,0) is a full confirmation; a holdout producing (1,1,0,0) is a partial confirmation; a holdout producing (0,1,\cdot,\cdot) is a falsification of the first component and therefore of the extension. The aggregated verdict follows the pre-registered threshold \theta_{\mathrm{holdout}} of §8.2. This is consistent with the six-valued classification of §3.7.

What ECC-H is not. It is not a statistical test — with n=1 training incident and a holdout sharing conditions, no confidence interval is available. It is a falsification attempt: a single holdout incident whose rupture is at the network layer with m=0 refutes the extension.

8.6 Status of v0.5.0

The present paper is ECC-R applied to Track A. The ECC-H program is stated as the next node; it is not executed here. Calling the present version "final" would conflate retrospective calibration with prospective validation.

