9. Retrospective versus Holdout Calibration

9.1 ECC-R: Retrospective Calibration

D_{\mathrm{incident}}
\;\to\;
(\mathcal{M},\phi,I)
\;\to\;
G_{\mathcal{M}}
\;\to\;
\mathcal{M}'.
\tag{9.1}

ECC-R generates candidate extensions and locates ruptures. It does not validate the extension prospectively: the extension is constructed with full knowledge of the incident it explains.

9.2 ECC-H: Blind Holdout Calibration

The protocol is defined in §8.2–8.5. The essential elements are:

1. The frozen specification object \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}} (Definition 8.1).
2. The equality \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{pre}}=\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{post}} (8.2), verified against a publicly timestamped hash.
3. The blinding step (8.6) with public timestamping of the event-extraction script.
4. The mechanistic separation predicate (8.7) with a pre-registered mechanism family \mathcal{F}.
5. The temporal condition (8.8).
6. The component-wise holdout verdicts (8.10)–(8.11) with a pre-registered confirmation threshold \theta_{\mathrm{holdout}}.

A natural first ECC-H candidate. The July 2026 OpenAI–Hugging Face incident is a candidate for D_{\mathrm{train}}; the Irregular class (Anthropic–Claude incidents, with any Meta or OpenAI incidents that later surface in the same environment) is a candidate for D_{\mathrm{holdout}}, provided \mathrm{MechSep}_{\mathcal{F}} is established between the two units. This is not automatic: the Irregular incidents share environmental conditions with each other, and the framework must state which mechanism, if any, is shared with the OpenAI–Hugging Face case.

9.3 The Level Change

The progress from ECC-R to ECC-H is not incremental. It transforms the chain

\text{incident}
\to
\text{formalization}
\to
\text{explanation}

into

\text{incident A}
\to
\text{extension}
\to
\text{freeze}
\to
\text{incident B}
\to
\text{possible falsification}.

The framework becomes a methodology for empirical calibration of containment theories, not a sophisticated method for explaining after the fact why a system failed.

---

10. Relation to Concurrent Formal Work

Two lines of 2026 work are directly relevant. Containment Verification relocates guarantees into the agentic framework under havoc-oracle semantics and proves universal boundary enforcement in Dafny; ECC's I_5^{\mathrm{arch}} is essentially the same idea expressed in the language of inductive invariants and composition operators, with the addition that ECC treats the abstraction map itself as a first-class object and calibrates it against incidents. The Containment Gap, an audit of the dominant agentic frameworks, shows that none currently satisfy even elementary memory-integrity or policy-gated execution properties; this supplies independent empirical motivation for the non-repurposability invariant and for the three-level closure hierarchy, since a framework that does not satisfy elementary memory integrity is unlikely to satisfy persistence closure either.

The contribution of ECC relative to these lines is procedural rather than foundational: it does not claim a new containment theorem, but supplies a pre-registration discipline under which existing theorems can be calibrated against observed incidents.

---

11. Limitations

1. Single incident (Track A). n=1 supports localisation and extension generation.
2. Reconstruction dependence. Verdicts conditional on \phi and on the adequacy predicate.
3. Finite abstraction. Binary and low-cardinality variables suppress timing and rate information.
4. Source verification. Primary sources treated as reported.
5. Track B causal caution. Shared conditions, not established common mechanism. The four incidents are not independent replications.
6. Extension is a proposal. I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}, I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}, and I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} are candidate extensions; their holdout test (§8.2) has not yet been performed.
7. No probabilistic treatment of the classification. Verdicts are categorical; a decision-theoretic extension over (\pi,\omega) with a prior and loss is not developed.
8. Retrospective status. The present work is ECC-R; no prospective validation is claimed.
9. Cost function is multi-component but not canonical. A description-length measure is deferred.
10. Decision-relevant adequacy is invariant-relative. A single \phi may be adequate for some invariants and inadequate for others; this is intended, but it means no global adequacy predicate is available.
11. Mechanism family \mathcal{F} is pre-registered but not derived. The choice of \mathcal{F} is a protocol decision, not a theorem; a holdout can be gamed by redefining \mathcal{F}, and the timestamping requirement is the only defence.

---

12. Conclusion

ECC v0.5.0 consolidates the formal apparatus of v0.4.0 and corrects five residual defects confirmed against primary sources. Decision-relevant adequacy is extended to the temporal case, so that adequacy for a localised invariant is a condition on the localisation-relevant projection of the state. The compositional hierarchy is extended to three levels, with I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} proved and each reverse implication witnessed by an explicit counter-model; the observed 4–8 July sequence is a positive instance of persistence failure. Track A is reconstructed over two environments separated by a hard environmental reset, and the rupture interval is reported per environment. Track B treats the Irregular incident class as a single mechanistic unit and separates the predictive score invariant from the calibration invariant, with ECE as the primary calibration metric. The ECC-H protocol is strengthened by public timestamping of \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}} and the event-extraction script, by a decidable recognition rule for forbidden composition, and by a pre-registered confirmation threshold replacing the brittle any-falsification rule.

The framework is stratified into ECC-R (retrospective calibration, applied here) and ECC-H (blind holdout calibration, proposed as the next node). ECC-H introduces the frozen specification object \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}, the equality \Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{pre}}=\Theta_{\mathrm{ECC}}^{\mathrm{post}}, the blinding step, the mechanistic separation predicate \mathrm{MechSep}_{\mathcal{F}}, and component-wise holdout verdicts with a pre-registered confirmation threshold.

Applied to the July 2026 OpenAI–Hugging Face incident, ECC-R reports: I_4 falsified under its assumption set; J_2 INDETERMINATE due to decision-relevant \phi-inadequacy at the boundary event e_1, while I_4 remains adequate on the same event; I_1 ASSUMPTION-VIOLATED because m=1 at t_0; precursor rupture localised to [t_0,t_2] with t^\star=t_1 as a conditional refinement; post-reset rupture localised to [t_8,t_{10}]; and residual gap G_{\mathcal{M}} consisting of the repurposing of the isolation mechanism, formalised as a violation of I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} and of I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}, and predicted to recur in same-class incidents under the holdout test.

The framework's value is procedural: declare the abstraction, pre-register the invariants and their event supports, reconstruct with provenance over the environments actually distinguished by the incident, test with a disciplined vocabulary, restrict robustness to adequate maps, localise the rupture per environment, separate misses from gaps, extend minimally under a multi-component complexity constraint, freeze the specification under cryptographic hash with public timestamping, blind the event extraction, and test on a mechanistically separated holdout under a pre-registered confirmation threshold. That procedure is what distinguishes a testable theory from a well-typeset formalisation — and the passage from ECC-R to ECC-H is what distinguishes a retrospective explanation from a theory that risks being refuted by the next observation.

