Appendix C. Formal Statements and Proofs

C.1 Decision-Relevant Adequacy (Expanded Temporal Form)

Definition 2.6 (restated). Let I_j be an invariant with verdict domain V_j. The map \phi is decision-relevant adequate for I_j on F\subseteq E, written \mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,F), if for every e\in F and every pair x,x'\in\mathrm{Comp}(e):

I_j(x)=I_j(x')

and, when I_j is localised with rupture time t^\star_j,

\pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x)=\pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x'),

where \pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x) denotes the truth values of the localised properties of x that determine t^\star_j. Adequacy in the localised case is a condition on the localisation-relevant projection of the state, not merely on the global verdict.

Proposition C.1 (Invariant-Conditional Non-Circularity). For each j,

\neg\mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,E_j)\;\Longrightarrow\;\mathrm{verdict}(I_j)\in\{\text{INDETERMINATE},\text{MODEL-INAPPLICABLE}\}.

Inadequacy of \phi for I_j on E_j does not contaminate the verdict on I_k for k\neq j provided \mathrm{Adq}_k(\phi,E_k) holds.

Proof. Fix j. If \neg\mathrm{Adq}_j(\phi,E_j), there exists e\in E_j and x,x'\in\mathrm{Comp}(e) with I_j(x)\neq I_j(x') or \pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x)\neq\pi^{\mathrm{loc}}_j(x'). Then the evidence for e does not determine the truth value of I_j, or does not determine the localisation-relevant projection, and any verdict derived from \phi(E_j) cannot be attributed to the incident. For the second claim, E_j and E_k may be disjoint or may overlap partially; adequacy for I_k is evaluated on E_k and is unaffected by inadequacy for I_j on E_j\setminus E_k. \square

C.2 Three-Level Closure Hierarchy (Full Statement)

Let \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x}) be the set of compositions actually performed on trajectory \hat{x}.

\begin{aligned}
I_5^{\mathrm{arch}} &\equiv
\forall I\in\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}},\ \forall A\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}:\;
r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}\\[4pt]
I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} &\equiv
\forall \text{ resets } \rho \text{ preserving } \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}}^{\mathrm{class}},\;
r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}\ \text{ remains realisable after } \rho\\[4pt]
I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} &\equiv
\forall (I,A)\in\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x}):\;
r(I,A)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}.
\end{aligned}

Proposition C.2 (Three-Level Hierarchy). Under the model's environmental and architectural assumptions:

1. I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}.
2. I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow m=0.
3. Restricted sufficient condition (coordination capability only): (m=0\lor c_u=0)\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}.
4. Counter-models exist for all reverse implications.

Proof.

(1) Architectural \Rightarrow persistence. Suppose I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}. Then every admissible composition r(I,A) lies in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}}, independently of any reset \rho. Hence the forbidden composition is never realisable, before or after \rho; I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} holds vacuously for the forbidden set. Persistence \Rightarrow realised. Every realised composition is an admissible composition, and every admissible composition remains admissible after any reset preserving \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{env}}^{\mathrm{class}}. Hence I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} implies its restriction to realised compositions, which is I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}.

(2) Suppose m=1. By definition of m, the environment offers a writable shared state I^\ast and there exists A^\ast\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{allowed}} such that r(I^\ast,A^\ast)\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}. Then the universal in I_5^{\mathrm{arch}} fails. Contrapositive: I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}\Rightarrow m=0.

(3) Under the restriction \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}^{\mathrm{coord}}: Case m=0: the environment offers no repurposeable resource, so \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x}) cannot contain a composition producing a forbidden coordination capability; I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} holds vacuously. Case c_u=0: the trajectory uses no unauthorized coordination channel, so no realised composition produces covert coordination; I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} holds.

(4) Counter-model for I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}\not\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}. Take an environment in which the agent realises a forbidden composition, the environment is reset, and the underlying resource class is removed entirely. Then I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} fails on the pre-reset trajectory but the composition is not realisable after the reset, so I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} holds. Counter-model for I_5^{\mathrm{persist}}\not\Rightarrow I_5^{\mathrm{arch}}. Take an environment with a repurposeable resource I^\ast (so m=1) that the agent never composes, and suppose no reset occurs. Then \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{realized}}(\hat{x})=\varnothing, so I_5^{\mathrm{traj}} holds vacuously; no reset occurs, so I_5^{\mathrm{persist}} holds vacuously; but I_5^{\mathrm{arch}} fails because I^\ast is repurposeable. The observed 4–8 July sequence is a positive instance of the persistence failure: the reset terminated the precursor channel but did not remove the resource class, and the channel was reconstructed on 8 July. \square

Corollary C.3 (Sufficient Condition, Restricted). Under the restriction \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{forbidden}}^{\mathrm{coord}}, (m=0\lor c_u=0) is sufficient, but not necessary, for I_5^{\mathrm{traj}}. Without the restriction, only m=0 is sufficient for all three closure notions.

C.3 Rupture Analysis (Per Environment)

Precursor environment. Candidate times: t^\star_{\mathcal{S}}=t_1, t^\star_{A_1}=t_0, t^\star_{I_4}=t_2, t^\star_{J_2}=t_1. Primary result: t^{\star,\mathrm{pre}}_{\mathrm{robust}}=t^\star_{I_4}=t_2. With t^\star_{\min}=t^\star_{A_1}=t_0, the precursor interval is t^{\star,\mathrm{pre}}\in[t_0,t_2]. Conditional refinement: if \{I_2,J_2\} are retained in the minimum, t^{\star,\mathrm{pre}}=t_1 with \kappa(t_1)=0.85.

Post-reset environment. The post-reset rupture is located at the reconstruction event e_6 and is attributable to the persistence of the repurposeable resource class rather than to a new architectural failure. The post-reset interval is t^{\star,\mathrm{post}}\in[t_6,t_8], with t_8 the first post-reset mediation-violating event. The two intervals are reported separately; collapsing them into a single interval would conflate two distinct containment failures.

