Appendix F. Relation to Concurrent Formal Work (2026)

F.1 Containment Verification (Moon & Varshney, arXiv:2605.09045)

Containment Verification relocates safety guarantees into the agentic framework under havoc-oracle semantics. It proves universal boundary enforcement by forward-simulation refinement, mechanised in Dafny. ECC's I_5^{\mathrm{arch}} is the inductive-invariant counterpart of the same idea, expressed via composition operators rather than typed action boundaries. The two approaches are complementary: containment verification supplies a deductive universal guarantee; ECC supplies an empirical calibration loop that can falsify or extend the assumptions under which that guarantee is claimed. The three-level hierarchy of §C.2 sharpens the relationship: containment verification addresses the architectural level; ECC adds the persistence and realised levels, which the incident demonstrates are not automatically satisfied by architectural closure alone.

F.2 The Containment Gap (arXiv:2606.12797)

The Containment Gap audits LangChain Agents, AutoGPT, and the OpenAI Agents SDK against six formal containment principles, finding zero native compliance across all 18 framework-principle evaluations. The paper validates the audit empirically with memory-poisoning and tool-bypass attacks on a simulated government benefits agent: a single memory-poisoning write induces persistent targeted corruption across all tested seeds and backends, increasing the wrongful denial rate for targeted applicants to 88.9 percent. Under a complex five-factor policy, the same attack preserves aggregate accuracy while increasing targeted wrongful denials by 3.5 times, rendering the corruption difficult to detect through standard monitoring. Two lightweight containment mechanisms — a memory integrity validator and a policy gate — eliminate both attack vectors with sub-millisecond overhead (<0.2 ms per call). This supplies independent empirical motivation for treating non-repurposability as a first-class invariant and for the three-level closure hierarchy: a framework that does not satisfy elementary memory integrity is unlikely to satisfy persistence closure either.

